from real import Real
from order_set import closed_interval_set
from analysis.topology.borel_functions import TopologicalSpace

// The Heine–Borel theorem (Eduard Heine, 1872; Émile Borel, 1895): a
// subset of Euclidean space is compact exactly when it is closed and
// bounded.  On the real line the theorem takes the classical open-cover
// form: every family of open sets covering a closed bounded interval
// `[a, b]` has a finite subfamily that still covers it.  The theorem is
// the topological compactness property of the real numbers; together with
// Bolzano–Weierstrass it is one of the two standard formulations of the
// completeness of the reals.  It underlies the uniform continuity of
// continuous functions on closed intervals, the existence of extrema, the
// Lebesgue number lemma, and the convergence of the Riemann sums of
// continuous functions.
//
// The formal statement below is the open-cover form in the library's
// topology: `is_compact[Real](closed_interval_set(a, b))` says that every
// open cover of the closed interval `[a, b]` admits a finite subcover.
// The library already has the closed-and-bounded form of compactness for
// real sets (in `real/topology_compact.ac`: a closed bounded real set is
// compact there), but that file's `is_compact_real_set` is closed-and-
// bounded *by definition*, and the open-cover property for intervals —
// the actual content of Heine–Borel — is not yet proved.
//
// Proof sketch (the classical bisection argument): suppose an open cover
// `c` of `[a, b]` has no finite subcover.  Then at least one of the halves
// `[a, m]`, `[m, b]` of the midpoint `m = (a + b)/2` also has no finite
// subcover (the union of two finite subcovers would be one for the whole
// interval).  Iterating produces a nested sequence of closed intervals
// `[a_n, b_n]` with lengths `(b - a)/2^n`, none finitely coverable.  By
// Cantor's intersection theorem the intervals have a common point `x`,
// which lies in some open set `U` of the cover; since `U` is open it
// contains a neighbourhood of `x`, and for large `n` the interval
// `[a_n, b_n]` lies inside it — a single-element subcover, contradicting
// the choice of the intervals.  The library has Cantor's intersection
// theorem (see `theorem_cantor_intersection.ac`) and the open-set
// machinery of `analysis/topology/`, but the bisection construction over
// dyadic subdivisions of an arbitrary interval is not yet formalized.

